    content:
      'The best way to remind a coworker without being awkward is a short, polite message that names exactly what you are waiting for and when you need it. Start with a greeting, name the task, give the reason the timing matters, and offer help in case they are busy.\n\nBelow are 6 WhatsApp messages you can copy and adjust. They are written for everyday work situations in an office or a small business.\n\n## 1. How do you remind someone about data they promised?\n\nThis is the most common case. A coworker says "I\'ll send it later", then forgets because the chat gets buried in a busy group.\n\nRepeat their promise using the same words. That way they remember right away without scrolling back.\n\nExample message: "Hi Dimas, just a reminder about the October sales data you said you\'d send. I need it before 3pm today for my report to Bu Rina. Thanks!"\n\n## 2. How do you chase a revision without sounding pushy?\n\nWhen you are waiting for a revised document or design, explain why you need it. People move faster when they know the purpose.\n\nAvoid lines like "why isn\'t it done yet?" because they sound like blame. Use a light, curious tone instead.\n\nExample message: "Hi Lia, how is the revision of the client proposal going? The client wants it tomorrow morning, so it would help to get it tonight. If any part is unclear, just let me know."\n\n## 3. What should you write when the deadline has passed?\n\nWhen a deadline has passed, don\'t scold right away. Your coworker may be overloaded or stuck on something they haven\'t mentioned.\n\nAsk about the problem first, then agree on a new time together. A new deadline they pick themselves is more likely to be met.\n\nExample message: "Hi Pak Hendra, the stock report planned for yesterday hasn\'t come in yet. Is something blocking it that I can help with? If you need more time, which day works for you?"\n\n## 4. How do you remind people in a work group without embarrassing anyone?\n\nReminding one person in a big group can feel like a public telling-off. When you can, send it as a private chat.\n\nIf it has to go in the group, for example because many people owe the same thing, keep it neutral and addressed to everyone.\n\nExample message: "Hi everyone, a quick reminder: this week\'s attendance recap is due by Friday at 12 noon. If you\'ve already sent yours, thank you!"\n\n## 5. How do you remind your manager or a senior colleague?\n\nReminding a manager often feels risky. The key is to frame the message as help to keep work moving, not as a demand.\n\nOffer something that makes their decision easier, such as preparing a draft for them.\n\nExample message: "Hi Bu Sari, sorry to bother you. Did you get a chance to review the event budget for next month? If it helps, I can prepare a one-page summary to speed things up."\n\n## 6. How do you send a second reminder?\n\nSometimes one reminder is not enough. The second message can be firmer, but it should stay polite and still offer a way out.\n\nState the impact honestly and suggest a small next step. Don\'t stack reminders within the same hour; give a fair gap.\n\nExample message: "Hi Bayu, following up again on the price file from the supplier. Without it I can\'t send the quote to our client. If you\'re swamped, feel free to send the raw version and I\'ll tidy it up."\n\n## Why do we forget who still owes us what?\n\nThe problem is not only coworkers who forget. We also lose track of who promised to send what, especially when those promises are spread across many groups.\n\nMake it a habit to note every promise someone makes to you as soon as it appears in a chat: who, what and when. A phone note, a spreadsheet or a task app all work.\n\nOne option is NANTI, a web app that reads WhatsApp chats you copy and paste, or chat screenshots you upload. NANTI notes who promised what and by when, then puts it on a Waiting page so you know when it\'s time to send a reminder.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Name the task, the deadline and the reason in one short message.\n- Ask questions instead of placing blame.\n- When a deadline passes, ask what\'s blocking it and agree on a new time.\n- Remind people in private chats unless the reminder is for everyone.\n- With managers, offer help that makes their decision easier.\n- Note coworkers\' promises as soon as they appear in a chat so you know when to follow up.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How long should I wait before reminding a coworker?\nIf there is a deadline, remind them a few hours before it. If there is no deadline, wait one or two working days, then ask politely.\n\n### Is it okay to send a reminder in a WhatsApp group?\nYes, if the reminder is for several people. For one person, a private chat is more polite and avoids embarrassing them.\n\n### What if my coworker still doesn\'t reply?\nAsk in person or make a quick call. If the task matters to the whole team, raise it with your manager using facts, not complaints.\n\n### Is "just following up" too pushy for a manager?\nNo, as long as you start with a greeting and add a short reason. A line of context makes the message feel helpful rather than demanding.',
