    image: "/blog/covers/whatsapp-time-management-tips-small-business.webp",
    content:
      'The best way to manage your time on WhatsApp as a small business owner is to answer chats in set time blocks, not every time your phone buzzes. Set your business hours and an away message, save quick replies for common questions, and write down every promise you make in a chat.\n\nCustomers still feel looked after, and you get real hours back for production, stock and bookkeeping. Here are 6 tips you can start using this week.\n\n## 1. Why should you reply to chats in time blocks?\n\nIf you answer every message the moment it arrives, your other work keeps getting cut short. Packing waits, the books stay half done, and you feel busy all day without much to show for it.\n\nTry three or four reply blocks a day, for example 9am, 12pm, 3pm and 7pm. Between blocks, focus on other work and let chats wait a little.\n\nMost customers are fine waiting an hour or two, as long as they know their message arrived. Tip 2 takes care of that.\n\n## 2. How do you set business hours and an away message?\n\nIn the WhatsApp Business app, you can add your opening hours to your business profile. Customers can see when you are open before they even send a message.\n\nThen turn on the away message. It goes out automatically when someone writes outside the hours you set, so nobody feels ignored.\n\nExample message: "Hi, thanks for contacting Sekar Batik. We are closed right now. We will reply tomorrow from 9am."\n\nIf you use regular WhatsApp, put your reply hours in your profile info. It is simple, but it helps customers know what to expect.\n\n## 3. How can you answer the same questions faster?\n\nMany customer chats ask the same few things: price, shipping cost, how to order and payment details. Typing the same answer again and again eats up your day.\n\nWhatsApp Business has quick replies. You save an answer once, then call it up by typing a slash and a shortcut, such as "/shipping" or "/payment".\n\nExample message: "Shipping cost depends on the package weight and your address. Could you send your full address? We will tell you the total before you pay."\n\nStart with the three to five answers you type most often. Add more when a new question keeps coming up.\n\n## 4. How do you decide which chats to answer first?\n\nNot every chat is equally urgent. A customer who has already paid and is waiting for confirmation should come before someone who is just browsing.\n\nUse labels in WhatsApp Business, such as "New order", "Not paid", "Paid" and "To ship". When a reply block starts, open the most urgent label first.\n\nYou can also pin ongoing chats, like your main supplier or a customer with a big order. They stay at the top and do not get buried.\n\n## 5. How do you stop group chats from eating your time?\n\nCommunity groups, supplier groups and family groups can send hundreds of messages a day. With every notification on, your focus keeps breaking.\n\nMute groups that do not need a fast answer and check them at set times. Keep notifications on only for groups that really matter, such as your shop admin group.\n\nWhen a group shares something important, like a supplier raising prices next week, write it down right away. Do not count on scrolling back later, because the message gets buried fast.\n\n## 6. How do you keep track of promises made in chats?\n\nWhen you reply quickly, you often make small promises like "I\'ll send the photos this afternoon" or "I\'ll check the stock tomorrow". These are the easiest things to forget.\n\nWrite each promise down the moment you make it. A calendar, a phone note or a notebook by the till all work, as long as it is one place you check every day.\n\nExample message: "Sure, Ms. Rina. I\'ll send you photos of the new patterns tomorrow at 10am."\n\nOne option is NANTI. You can paste a copied chat or upload a screenshot, and NANTI picks out who, what and when, then reminds you in the app. Whatever tool you use, your reply blocks feel calmer when promises are not only in your head.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Answer chats in three or four time blocks a day, not on every notification.\n- Add opening hours to your profile and turn on an away message.\n- Save the answers you type most often as quick replies.\n- Use labels and pinned chats so urgent customers get answered first.\n- Mute groups that do not need a fast reply and check them at set times.\n- Write down every promise you make in a chat right away.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Will customers leave if I don\'t reply right away?\nUsually not, as long as they know their message arrived and when you will answer. An away message and opening hours on your profile help with that.\n\n### Are quick replies available in regular WhatsApp?\nQuick replies, labels and away messages are features of the WhatsApp Business app. The app is free and works with your business number.\n\n### How many reply blocks should I have each day?\nThree or four blocks work for most small businesses. If chats spike at a certain hour, add one block at that time.\n\n### What if a chat is really urgent?\nAnswer it, then go back to your work. Reply blocks are a habit, not a strict rule.',
