    image: "/blog/covers/client-follow-up-messages-not-pushy.webp",
    content:
      "To follow up with a client on WhatsApp without sounding pushy, keep the message short, name the exact thing you are following up on, and ask one question that is easy to answer. Give a reasonable gap between messages. Here are 10 example messages for common situations, each with a note on when to send it.\n\n## 1. How do you follow up after sending a quote?\n\nSend it 2 to 3 working days after the quote. Mention the quote by name so the client doesn't have to scroll up.\n\nExample message: \"Hi Bu Rina, just checking if you had a chance to look at the quote for the 200 uniform shirts. If anything needs adjusting, let me know.\"\n\n## 2. What should you send after a meeting?\n\nSend it the same day while the conversation is fresh. Recap what you agreed on instead of only saying thanks.\n\nExample message: \"Thanks for your time today, Pak Budi. Quick recap: I'll send the revised design on Thursday, and you'll confirm the final quantity on Friday. Please correct me if I missed anything.\"\n\n## 3. How do you reach a client who went quiet?\n\nWait about a week. Give them an easy way out so they don't feel guilty about not replying.\n\nExample message: \"Hi Kak Dina, hope your week is going well. Is the product photo package still planned for this month, or should we push it back? Either is fine, I just want to plan my schedule.\"\n\n## 4. How do you ask for an approval you're waiting on?\n\nUse this when your work is stuck until the client signs off. Explain the effect on the timeline in a neutral tone.\n\nExample message: \"Pak Andi, version 2 of the packaging design is still waiting for your approval. If I hear back before Wednesday, production can still start on the 20th as planned.\"\n\n## 5. What do you ask after the client gets a sample?\n\nSend it 2 to 4 days after the sample arrives, once they've had time to try it. Ask something specific.\n\nExample message: \"Hi Bu Sari, have you had a chance to try the 150 gram sambal sample? I'd love to know if the spice level works for your customers.\"\n\n## 6. How do you re-contact an old client?\n\nGood for clients who haven't ordered in a few months. Bring a real reason, like a new product or a busy season, not just \"how are you\".\n\nExample message: \"Hi Pak Hendra, it's been a while. We're taking Lebaran hamper orders again. You ordered 50 packages last year, would you like me to send the new catalog?\"\n\n## 7. How do you remind a client about a missing down payment?\n\nSend it one day after the promised date. Don't accuse. Just check and resend the payment details.\n\nExample message: \"Kak Maya, I checked and the DP for the wedding invitation order hasn't come into our account yet. It might still be processing. Here are our bank details again to make it easy.\"\n\n## 8. What should you send when an invoice is overdue?\n\nUse this when an invoice is a few days past due. Attach the invoice again and offer help if something is blocking payment.\n\nExample message: \"Good morning Bu Lestari, invoice 0312 for last month's design work was due on the 5th. I've attached it again here. If anything is holding it up, just let me know.\"\n\n## 9. How do you follow up after a project is done?\n\nSend it one or two weeks after handover. The goal is to keep the relationship warm and open the door to the next order.\n\nExample message: \"Hi Mas Rudi, the shop website has been live for two weeks now. Any issues, or anything you'd like to add? Happy to help if needed.\"\n\n## 10. How do you close out a follow-up that gets no reply?\n\nAfter two or three unanswered messages, send a polite closing note. Clients often reply to this one.\n\nExample message: \"Pak Yoga, it seems the timing isn't right, so I won't keep bothering you. If you need a cardboard box supplier again, message me anytime.\"\n\n## A simple habit so follow-ups don't slip\n\nA good message doesn't help if you forget to send it. Try this routine:\n\n1. After every client chat, note what you're waiting for and from whom.\n2. Pick a follow-up date right away instead of \"later\".\n3. Check your follow-up list every morning before opening other groups.\n4. After following up, set the next date or mark it done.\n\nA notebook, a spreadsheet or an app all work. One option is NANTI: paste a copied WhatsApp chat or upload a screenshot, and NANTI notes who is waiting on what and when, puts it on the Waiting page, and sends in-app reminders.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Always give context: project name, quantity or invoice number.\n- Ask one question that's easy to answer.\n- Leave a sensible gap, usually 2 to 7 days depending on the situation.\n- Give the client a way out so they don't feel pressured.\n- Close politely after a few unanswered messages.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### How many times should I follow up with a client?\nUsually two or three times with growing gaps, then one closing message. After that, wait until you have a new reason to reach out.\n\n### Is it better to message or call?\nStart with a message so the client can reply when they're free. Call when it's urgent or when the client prefers phone calls.\n\n### What time should I send a follow-up?\nDuring working hours, such as around 9 in the morning or after lunch. Avoid late evenings and weekends unless the client usually replies then.\n\n### What if the client reads my message but doesn't reply?\nAssume they're busy. Wait a few days, then send a lighter question or give them clear options to pick from.",
