    content:
      "A polite payment reminder on WhatsApp names the invoice number, the amount and the due date, uses a respectful greeting, and ends with one easy next step, like \"just send the transfer receipt here once it's done.\" You can get firmer with each message, but never sarcastic or accusing. Below are 7 templates for each stage of chasing a payment, ready to copy and adjust.\n\n## 1. How do you remind a client before the due date?\n\nSend it two or three days early. The goal is not to chase but to make sure the invoice reached the right person. Invoices often get buried in the chat or never reach finance.\n\nExample message: \"Good morning Bu Rina, a quick heads-up that invoice INV-0412 for Rp4,500,000 (packaging design) is due this Friday, 14 November. If you'd like me to resend it or forward it to your finance team, just let me know. Thank you!\"\n\n## 2. What do you send on the due date itself?\n\nOn the day, keep it short and neutral. The client may be planning to transfer that afternoon.\n\nExample message: \"Hi Pak Dimas, invoice INV-0398 for Rp2,750,000 is due today. Payment can go to BCA 123-456-7890 under Studio Contoh. Once it's sent, feel free to drop the receipt here so I can mark it as paid. Many thanks.\"\n\n## 3. How do you follow up when a payment is a few days late?\n\nAfter three to five days, stay polite but ask a question that needs a real answer, so you get a date to work with.\n\nExample message: \"Hi Kak Sari, following up on invoice INV-0377 for Rp1,200,000, which was due on 5 November. Is anything holding up the payment? If you need a bit more time, could you let me know roughly when the transfer will go out? Thanks!\"\n\n## 4. How do you ask for the balance after a deposit?\n\nFor jobs paid with a DP (down payment), state what has been paid and what is still owed. This heads off confusion about the numbers.\n\nExample message: \"Hi Bu Ani, thank you for the Rp3,000,000 deposit on 20 October. Your 200 tumblers are ready to ship. The remaining Rp3,000,000 is due before delivery, as we agreed. As soon as it comes in, I'll book the courier the same day.\"\n\nTie the payment to something the client is waiting for, such as delivery or final files.\n\n## 5. What if the client promised to transfer and didn't?\n\nQuote their own promise with the date, without accusing. That means writing down every transfer promise when it's made.\n\nExample message: \"Good afternoon Pak Hendra, yesterday you mentioned the transfer for INV-0365 (Rp5,800,000) would go out on Monday. I haven't seen it come in yet. If it's already sent, could you share the receipt so I can check with my bank?\"\n\n## 6. How do you ask for proof of transfer without sounding suspicious?\n\nSometimes a client says \"already paid\" but the money hasn't shown up in your account. Ask for the receipt as an admin step, not as a sign of doubt.\n\nExample message: \"Thanks for letting me know, Kak Maya. Could you send a photo of the transfer receipt? I need it to match against my bank statement. I'll send a paid confirmation as soon as it's recorded.\"\n\nOnce the money lands, always confirm it.\n\n## 7. What does a firm but polite final reminder look like?\n\nAfter several weeks of unanswered messages, go more formal. List past reminders, set a new deadline and mention only consequences from your original agreement.\n\nExample message: \"Good morning Pak Yoga, I'm writing again about invoice INV-0341 for Rp7,500,000, which was due on 10 October. I sent reminders on 13, 20 and 27 October. Please settle the payment by Friday, 21 November at the latest. As agreed at the start of the project, I'll hold further revisions until this invoice is paid. I'm open to discussing instalments if needed. Thank you.\"\n\nOffering instalments gives a client short on cash an option other than going quiet.\n\n## How do you schedule all these reminders?\n\nUse this sequence for every invoice:\n\n1. When you send an invoice, write down the number, amount, client and due date in one place.\n2. Set a reminder for yourself three days before the due date, then send template 1.\n3. On the due date, send template 2.\n4. If there's no word three to five days later, send template 3.\n5. Whenever a client names a transfer date, write down the promise and the date.\n6. After three reminders with no result, send template 7.\n\nA notebook or spreadsheet works. If client promises are scattered across many chats, NANTI is one option: paste a copied chat or upload a screenshot, and it picks out who promised what and by when, so the payment shows up on the Waiting page as something you're expecting from someone else.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Always include the invoice number, amount, due date and bank account.\n- Send the first reminder before the due date, not after.\n- Raise the firmness step by step: remind, ask, then set a deadline.\n- Write down every transfer promise and quote it back when you follow up.\n- Send a paid confirmation once the money arrives.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What time of day is best for a payment reminder?\nWeekdays between about 9 am and 3 pm. Avoid evenings and weekends.\n\n### Should I call if the client ignores my WhatsApp messages?\nYes, after two or three unanswered messages. Then send a short WhatsApp summary so there's a written record.\n\n### What if the client asks for a discount when I chase the payment?\nDon't answer on the spot. Say you'll check, then decide based on the original agreement.\n\n### Is it okay to chase a payment in a WhatsApp group?\nBetter not. It can embarrass the client. Message the person who handles payments directly.",
